  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 331 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry through the week. Above normal (but \nstill seasonable) temperatures prevail through the end of the week \ninto next weekend. Uncertainty remains for the potential for some \nrain this weekend. \n\nEarly morning weather analysis weak upper level ridge over west-\ncentral Gulf coast to the Great Lakes and an upper trough sliding \noffshore. At the surface, a weak and dry cold front is located \nacross central PA while further to the north a 1032mb high pressure \nsits north of the Great Lakes. The pressure gradient ahead of the \napproaching front remains weak allowing for efficient decoupling. \nTemperatures are ranging between the low to middle 40s with some \nisolated upper 30s in the localized cooler areas. With relatively \nclear skies this morning and the weak gradient temperatures are \nlikely to continue to drop into the upper 30s to low 40s by sunrise. \n\nFor today, a weak and dry cold front is expected to drop south out \nof PA and move through the area. This will allow for winds to shift \nout of the N-NE with slightly higher winds forecasted along the \ncoast. The high behind the front will the become centered over the \narea late tonight and through Wednesday allowing temperatures to stay \nnear-normal. The high will then eventually anchor off the southern \nNew England to Mid-Atlantic coast Wednesday night and Thursday while \nlow pressure remains suppressed well south near the Bahamas. With \nthe high pressure offshore, SSW will allow for highs to potentially \nreach the the middle to upper 70s Thursday and Friday. \n\nDry weather conditions are forecasted to prevail through the late \nweekend, as upper ridging builds over the region. By Friday and into \nthe weekend, a northern stream shortwave traversing central Canada \nwill flatten the upper pattern. Halloween continues to look dry \npleasant, and mild with increasing clouds ahead of of a weak cold \nfront allowing for optimal Trick-or-Treating conditions across the \nregion. Looking Sunday into early next week there is the potential \nfor the pattern to become slightly unsettled. The latest 00z/27 \nensembles continue to hint on a weak system moving across the area \nduring the first couple days of November. However, at this time the \nensembles remain in heavy disagreement on amounts of precipitation. \nThe Euro, Canadian, and Euro-AI ensembles have roughly 40-60% of \nrainfall greater than .1\" while the GEFS is the highest with 80-90%. \nWith the disagreement this is leading to low confidence in beneficial \nrainfall this weekend and into early next week.",
